# Code Review

Reviewed commit: `9ea62bb`

## Scope

This review focused on the public API and admission boundary, protected ownership state,
project deletion and recovery, symbol-source credentials, and the Sentry-to-Telegram delivery
path. The repository is explicitly alpha, so deferred external-delivery evidence is not treated
as a defect. Findings below are ordered by severity.

## Findings

### [P1] Make claim and binding cleanup recoverable after either delete succeeds

`internal/project/engine.go:482-489` deletes the manager claim before deleting the project
binding. If the first delete succeeds and the second delete fails because of an API timeout,
conflict, or transient outage, the next Retain reconciliation cannot resume: `releaseManaged`
requires the claim before it checks the submitted `ReleaseOwnership` journal
(`internal/project/engine.go:422-439`). The controller consequently keeps the finalizer and
reports `OwnershipLost` forever (`internal/controller/sentryproject_controller.go:1877-1888`),
even though remote ownership has already been released.

There is a second instance of the same two-record problem during creation. Both managed create
and adoption acquire the claim before creating the binding
(`internal/project/engine.go:544-555` and `internal/project/engine.go:775-804`). If binding
creation fails and the CR is deleted before reconciliation repairs it, the Retain path treats a
missing binding as complete (`internal/project/engine.go:277-280`) without removing the claim.
That orphan blocks every later CR with the same installation, organization, and slug because
`AcquireClaim` rejects a different project UID (`internal/binding/store.go:277-299`). With
`DeleteProject`, the missing binding instead leaves the deleting CR blocked.

Persist an explicit protected-state cleanup phase or make deletion recover both partial states:
same-UID claim without binding, and submitted binding without claim. Add fault-injection tests
for failure after claim creation and after claim deletion; the current happy-path and lost remote
response tests do not exercise either local persistence boundary.

### [P1] Do not map raw Sentry events directly to every configured Telegram alert

The hook handler deliberately retains only the project slug and event ID
(`internal/sentryhook/handler.go:123-138`). `telegramHookDeliveries` then converts every enabled
Telegram-backed alert into a static route without preserving its trigger, window, threshold, or
Sentry rule identity (`internal/controller/sentryproject_controller.go:2002-2025`). Finally, the
producer enqueues every resolved route for every accepted event
(`internal/sentryhook/producer.go:55-85`).

If this path is enabled, a `first-seen`, `regression`, `event-count`, and `user-count` alert all
behave identically: each individual project event is sent to every Telegram route. In particular,
the count triggers cannot be implemented from the current envelope because their window and
threshold are unavailable. This would produce false notifications and make the public alert
configuration misleading.

Keep the path unavailable until the input is an authenticated notification that identifies an
actual Sentry alert firing, or implement a durable evaluator that has the required trigger state.
An end-to-end test should submit events that do and do not satisfy each trigger and assert the
exact routes enqueued.

### [P2] Align the public Telegram selector schema with admission behavior

The CRD-facing `AlertSpec.Notify` validation explicitly accepts `telegram/...`
(`api/v1alpha1/sentryproject_types.go:704-710`), but both admission paths reject every Telegram
alert destination (`internal/webhook/v1alpha1/sentryproject_webhook.go:395-402` and
`internal/webhook/v1alpha1/sentryalertpolicy_webhook.go:68-75`). The project webhook test confirms
that this rejection is intentional (`internal/webhook/v1alpha1/sentryproject_webhook_test.go:204-225`).
At the same time, the controller contains service-hook lifecycle and route construction code for
Telegram alerts. The result is a contradictory public contract: generated schema and clients
advertise a selector which no valid admitted alert can use, while controller tests can exercise a
state that users cannot create through the normal API.

Until the trigger-semantics issue above is resolved, remove `telegram` from the public `notify`
pattern and state the limitation next to the field. When Telegram alerts become supported, change
the schema and both webhooks together and add an admission-enabled controller test so invalid
direct-client fixtures cannot be mistaken for a reachable feature.

### [P2] Reject credentials embedded in HTTP symbol-source URLs

`HTTPSymbolSource` says that a password can only come from a namespace-local Secret, but its URL
pattern accepts values such as `https://user:password@example.test/symbols`
(`api/v1alpha1/sentryproject_types.go:321-334`). Admission validates only the optional
`passwordSecretRef` (`internal/webhook/v1alpha1/sentryproject_webhook.go:293-320`), and the domain
validator accepts any parsed HTTPS URL with a host (`internal/symbolsource/engine.go:245-253`).
The original URL is then sent to Sentry as part of the symbol-source request. This lets a password
or token bypass the Secret-reference boundary and be stored in a public CR, Git history, and the
remote Sentry configuration.

Reject `parsed.User != nil` in admission and in the domain validator, matching the existing
defense for `SentryConnection` URLs. Add tests for userinfo and consider rejecting fragments and
queries unless they are an intentional, documented part of the symbol-source contract.

## Verification

Passed locally:

- `make test`
- `make test-matrix` (Kubernetes 1.35 and 1.36)
- `make lint`
- `make verify-english`
- `git diff --check`

The first sandboxed `make test` attempt could not bind envtest/HTTP listener ports because of the
sandbox policy; the identical command passed outside that restriction. No kind, Docker, live
Sentry, public receiver, or external notification-delivery run was performed. Therefore this
review does not claim event-ingestion, browser, or Telegram/Slack/PagerDuty delivery evidence.
